
awmaker — Abstracting Window Maker
==================================
A Window Maker fork
Rodolfo García Peñas (kix) — 2014

awmaker is a fork of the GNU Window Maker window manager whose long-term
goal is to abstract itself away from X11 as much as possible, removing
hard dependencies on the X server so that screens/monitors can be added
and removed without restarting the window manager.

The shared libraries (WINGs, WUtil, WRaster/wrlib, wmlib) and the WPrefs
application are shared with upstream. awmaker's own `src/` core and its
configuration behaviour evolve independently.


Why?
====
Window Maker is a nice window manager, but it is screen dependent: we
cannot run it without a screen, and — more importantly — every item it
creates (icons, windows, the Dock, menus...) is bound to the screen it
was created on. If you connect another screen, Window Maker has to be
restarted to repaint everything on the new screen.

awmaker changes that model. When an item is created, it is not mapped
to a screen; it is only created. When the user needs it, it is mapped to
the current screen. When the user is done, it is unmapped (not destroyed,
only unmapped). Screen-related state is built on the fly, so a new screen
can be used without restarting.

That is the "Abstraction" in awmaker: decouple the item lifecycle from
any particular screen, as preparation for hot-pluggable monitors.


User-facing features
====================
Beyond the upstream Window Maker behaviour, awmaker bundles the
following improvements. These are the interesting changes from the
point of view of an everyday user.

Configuration is created automatically
--------------------------------------
awmaker creates its own configuration files **in code** when they are
missing on disk, on first launch. There is no need to run a separate
installer. The following files are written only when absent, under
`~/GNUstep/Defaults/`:

- `WindowMaker`   — the window manager defaults domain (small, editable
  subset; anything not listed falls back to the binary's built-ins)
- `WMGLOBAL`      — global WINGs/font/mouse domain
- `WMRootMenu`    — the full root menu
- `WMState`       — the Dock, a Clip, empty Drawers and the `Main` workspace
- `WMWindowAttributes`

It also creates two user-editable scripts under
`~/GNUstep/Library/WindowMaker/`:

- `autostart`   — run automatically when awmaker starts
- `exitscript`  — run automatically when awmaker exits

A complete root menu
--------------------
The default root menu is a full property-list menu with these submenus:
**Info**, **Applications**, **Utils**, **Selection**, **Commands**,
**Appearance** (solid / gradient / image backgrounds, plus Styles,
Themes and IconSets) and **Session**. It is fully editable.

Unified keybindings with multi-key chains
-----------------------------------------
Every keybinding — the built-in window-manager shortcuts *and* the
root-menu command shortcuts — is handled through one unified model.

You can bind a **key chain** (a sequence of key combinations) to a single
action. For example:

    RootMenuKey = Control+Alt+a Control+Alt+b

pressed in sequence, launches the action bound to that key. Each binding
accepts up to 8 tokens (1 leader + 7 followers). An in-progress chain can
be cancelled (`KeychainCancelKey`) and expires after a delay
(`KeychainTimeoutDelay`).

Key Bindings panel
------------------
awmaker exposes a **Key Bindings** panel that lists the assigned
keybindings (sorted by key). It can be launched directly from the root
menu and can itself be bound to a keyboard shortcut.

Configuring with WPrefs
-----------------------
The `WPrefs` configurator offers two complementary editors:

- the **Keyboard Shortcuts** tab — for the built-in window-manager
  keybindings;
- the **menu editor** — for the shortcuts of the commands in the root
  menu (it serialises multi-key chains such as
  `Control+Alt+a Control+Alt+b`).

The menu editor also provides **internal commands** (**Info**, **Legal**,
and the new **Open Key Bindings Panel**), so a generated root menu can be
round-tripped through WPrefs without discarding entries. Dropped internal
commands get a descriptive title.

Dock / Clip / Drawer
--------------------
Because screens can be hot-plugged, the Dock, Clip and Drawer — the three
"container" elements — were split into a shared core (`dock-core`) and
per-type modules, so the common logic is separated from each type's
behaviour. This is groundwork for the X11-abstraction work described
below.

Shared libraries via the wmaker-crm submodule
---------------------------------------------
The common libraries (WINGs, WUtil, WRaster) are no longer maintained
inside awmaker; their source of truth is the `wmaker-crm` git submodule
(`third_party/wmaker-crm`). This keeps the two projects in sync and lets
awmaker's own `src/` core evolve independently. See "Building
dependencies" below for how they are consumed at build time.



Compiling and installing
========================
awmaker is built and installed with the standard GNU autotools workflow
(autoconf / automake / configure / make), both when compiling from
sources and when packaging for a distribution:

```sh
./autogen.sh      # generates the configure script (only needed from a git checkout)
./configure       # configures the build for your system
make              # builds the binaries
make install      # installs awmaker system-wide (as root, when packaging)
```

Alternatively, install awmaker from a pre-built package provided by your
distribution (e.g. an `.rpm` or `.deb`), which is the normal way to deploy
it on a production system. See `INSTALL-WMAKER` for the detailed upstream
options and `./configure --help` for the full list of configure options.

> awmaker is a window manager and must be the *only* WM on the display,
> so you start it yourself (e.g. from your X session) after install.

Building dependencies
---------------------
The common libraries (WINGs, WUtil, WRaster) come from the
`third_party/wmaker-crm` git submodule and are compiled out-of-tree into a
local `build-libs/` prefix; awmaker consumes them via pkg-config. When
building awmaker, set `PKG_CONFIG_PATH` accordingly, or let your
distribution package handle the dependencies.

Development only: Docker
------------------------
The `make docker` / `make docker-run` targets (top-level `GNUmakefile`)
build and run awmaker inside a container with a read-write mount of the
source tree. They exist to **develop** on systems where the X11/autotools
toolchain is impractical (notably macOS), and reproduce the autotools
build above.

> The Docker targets are **for development only** — not for executing
> awmaker in production. For production, install with `make install`
> or with a distribution package as described above.
>
> The containerised build still runs `./autogen.sh`, `./configure` and
> `make`; it just does so inside the container (see
> `script/docker-build.sh`).

```sh
# one-time setup (on the host)
git submodule update --init third_party/wmaker-crm

make docker                # build shared libs + awmaker inside a container
make docker-clean          # clean generated outputs

# run inside the container, drawing on the macOS XQuartz server
make docker-run            # build + run awmaker on XQuartz
make docker-run --no-build # run only (assumes already built)
```

Notes for the Docker development workflow:
- `make docker` regenerates the generated files (`Makefile.in`,
  `README.i18n`, `INSTALL-WMAKER`, ...) via `./autogen.sh`. These are
  written back into the host tree (they are not committed).
- To run the binary from a terminal, set `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` to the local
  lib prefix (`build-libs/lib`).
- On macOS, switch XQuartz to **Full Screen** (`View -> Full Screen`,
  `Cmd-Option-A`) before expecting a faithful WM layout; `make docker-run`
  handles the XQuartz display setup and assets for you.


The whole-screen abstraction (current work)
===========================================
The active work is the abstraction of screens: removing the X11 coupling
so screens/monitors can be added and removed **without restarting the
WM**. All functional and robustness bugs are resolved; the abstraction is
the current focus.

Today screen enumeration happens once at startup and there is no runtime
add/remove path (a screen change triggers a full WM restart). The planned
phases introduce a configurable virtual-screen count, a teardown path,
RandR hot-plug refresh, per-element presentation backends (root menu
first, then dock/clip/drawer) and, finally, a per-screen display handle.
Reported as WB-05, CFG-02 and ABST-01..03.


Contributing
============
Fork it and send a pull request: we are at
https://github.com/awmaker/awmaker

- Please read `TODO.md` for the open work and operational notes if you
  are working on the tree.
- Follow the repo's commit style: a short imperative subject prefixed by
  the touched area (e.g. `src/clip.c:`, `TODO.md:`).
- Do not `git push` unless asked; leave pushing to the maintainer.


